@page "/mcp-server"

@* Describes the MCP server this demo's host maps at /mcp. Its route differs from the endpoint's on
   purpose: two literal endpoints on /mcp would make a GET of it ambiguous. *@

<PageTitle>MCP server · Balette</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>MCP server</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        This site hosts a Model Context Protocol server at <code>/mcp</code>, so an AI agent adding a command palette with
        Bit.Balette reads the library itself - its compiled API documentation, its guide, its stylesheet's variables, its
        real matcher and these demo pages - instead of guessing at an API it half remembers. Every tool is read-only.
    </p>
</div>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Connect a client</h3>

    <p>
        The endpoint is plain Streamable HTTP, so any MCP client connects with a URL and nothing else.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock Language="json" Code="@ClientConfig" />
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Tools</h3>

    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it answers</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key"><code>SearchBalette</code></td>
                    <td>Everything at once - the guide, every public member, the theme variables, the setup guides and the demo sources - each hit with the exact follow-up call.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key"><code>GetBaletteSetupGuide</code></td>
                    <td>The complete wiring for one render mode: server, wasm, auto, standalone-wasm or hybrid.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key"><code>GetBaletteGuideSection</code></td>
                    <td>One section of the reference guide, or the index of sections.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key"><code>GetBaletteApi</code></td>
                    <td>One public type with every parameter, property and method, its real default and its documentation - or the index of types.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key"><code>GetBaletteThemeVariables</code></td>
                    <td>Every <code>--bit-balette-*</code> custom property with its light and dark value, and the <code>data-balette</code> attributes to style an unstyled palette by.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key"><code>InspectBaletteSearch</code></td>
                    <td>Runs the library's own matcher and ranker over a query and a list of commands, and reports each score, the highlighted positions and the final order.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key"><code>GetBaletteSourceFile</code></td>
                    <td>One demo or library file verbatim - every page here is a working palette feature - or the index of files.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <p>
        Called without a key, the <code>Get…</code> tools answer with their index - the setup guides, the guide's sections, the
        types, the files - to pick from.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Resources and prompts</h3>
    <p>The guide, the API, the theme variables and the sources are also exposed as resources:</p>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Resource</th><th>Holds</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>balette://guide</code></td><td>The reference guide, whole</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>balette://guide/{heading}</code></td><td>One section of it</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>balette://api</code></td><td>The public API's index</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>balette://api/{typeName}</code></td><td>One type, with its members</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>balette://theme</code></td><td>The theme variables</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>balette://source/{path}</code></td><td>One demo or library file</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>And three prompts walk an agent through the common jobs in the right order:</p>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Prompt</th><th>Takes</th><th>Does</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>add-balette-to-app</code></td><td><code>renderMode</code>: server, wasm, auto, standalone-wasm, hybrid or unknown</td><td>Adds Bit.Balette to an existing app, in the right order for its render mode - working the mode out first when it is unknown.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>implement-balette-feature</code></td><td><code>feature</code>, in your own words</td><td>Implements commands, nested pages, prompts, multi-select, modes, sources, shortcuts or theming with the real API.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>debug-balette-palette</code></td><td><code>symptom</code></td><td>Diagnoses <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>K</kbd> doing nothing, an unstyled palette, a missing, oddly ranked or stale command, a silent shortcut or an empty source.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</section>

